    "api_error_status": null,
    "result": "Perfect! I've fixed the floating-point precision bug. Here's what was wrong and what I changed:\n\n## The Problem\n\nWhen converting decimal prices to cents by multiplying by 100, JavaScript's floating-point arithmetic causes precision errors. For example:\n- `39.95 * 100` should equal `3995`, but instead gives `3995.0000000000005`\n- The validation checked `Number.isInteger(price)`, which failed on this non-integer value\n\nNetflix and Spotify worked because `12.99 * 100` and `10.99 * 100` happen to work out correctly due to floating-point luck.\n\n## The Solution\n\nI added `Math.round()` to three functions in the money/locale code:\n\n1. **src/money.js** - `parseEuros()`: Added rounding when parsing decimal-point format (like \"39.95\")\n2. **src/money.js** - `cellCents()`: Added rounding when parsing spreadsheet cells\n3. **src/locale.js** - `decimalCommaToCents()`: Added rounding when parsing decimal-comma format (like \"39,95\")\n\n## Verification\n\n\u2705 All 50 existing tests pass  \n\u2705 Successfully added \"Gym 39.95\" subscription  \n\u2705 Correctly stored as 3995 cents in JSON\n\nThe fix is ready to commit whenever you'd like!",
